    "pathway_role_summary": "CTNNB1 is the central signal transducer in the canonical Wnt signaling pathway. In the absence of Wnt ligand, beta-catenin is sequestered in a cytoplasmic destruction complex (containing APC, AXIN1/2, GSK3B, and CSNK1A1), where it undergoes sequential phosphorylation at Ser45 (by CK1) followed by Thr41, Ser37, and Ser33 (by GSK3B). This phosphorylation creates a recognition motif for the beta-TrCP E3 ubiquitin ligase, leading to ubiquitination and proteasomal degradation. Upon Wnt ligand binding to Frizzled receptors and LRP5/6 co-receptors, the destruction complex is inhibited, allowing beta-catenin to accumulate and translocate to the nucleus. There, it binds TCF/LEF transcription factors and recruits coactivators (BCL9, CBP/p300) to activate Wnt target genes involved in development, stem cell maintenance, and proliferation. Beta-catenin also plays a structural role in adherens junctions through binding to cadherins and alpha-catenin. Dysregulation through activating mutations or destruction complex inactivation drives multiple cancers, particularly colorectal carcinoma."
  },
