    "data_quality_notes": "Comprehensive data available from all major databases. UniProt entry is well-curated with extensive PTM annotations. Multiple high-resolution crystal structures available showing different functional states and binding partners. AlphaFold prediction shows expected pattern with high confidence for structured armadillo repeat domain and lower confidence for intrinsically disordered N- and C-terminal regions. Interaction data is extensive (2779+ interactors documented) with high-confidence experimental validation for key pathway components. Some newer PDB structures (2024 deposits: 8Y14, 8Y0G, 8Z0U, 8Z10, 8Z61) lack detailed annotation in accessible databases. IntAct specific interaction details required direct database access beyond web scraping capabilities. RefSeq isoform sequences not fully enumerated but appear to be primarily transcript variants encoding the same protein product.",
    "modeling_relevance_summary": "CTNNB1/beta-catenin is essential for Wnt pathway modeling as the central signal transducer. Key modeling considerations: (1) N-terminal phosphorylation cascade (Ser45 priming by CK1, followed by sequential GSK3B phosphorylation at Thr41/Ser37/Ser33) creates destruction motif; (2) Phospho-dependent ubiquitination by beta-TrCP E3 ligase determines protein half-life; (3) Nuclear translocation and TCF/LEF binding activates transcription; (4) Competing roles in adherens junctions vs. transcription; (5) Multiple positive and negative feedback loops through target genes (e.g., AXIN2); (6) Disease-relevant mutations at destruction box residues provide constitutive activation phenotypes for modeling oncogenic signaling; (7) Extensive interaction network with destruction complex components (APC, AXIN1/2, GSK3A/B, CSNK1A1), transcriptional machinery (TCF/LEF family, BCL9, EP300/CREBBP), and structural components (cadherins, catenins) enables systems-level pathway reconstruction."
